"eos","text":"To simplify the expression $\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 30^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ \\cos 70^\\circ$, we can use trigonometric identities and properties.\n\nStep 1: Use the known value of $\\cos 30^\\circ$.\n\\[\n\\cos 30^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\nThus, the expression becomes:\n\\[\n\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ \\cos 70^\\circ \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nStep 2: Use the product-to-sum identities to simplify $\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ \\cos 70^\\circ$.\n\nWe can use the identity:\n\\[\n\\cos A \\cos B = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos(A+B) + \\cos(A-B)]\n\\]\n\nFirst, let's simplify the product $\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 70^\\circ$:\n\\[\n\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 70^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos(10^\\circ + 70^\\circ) + \\cos(10^\\circ - 70^\\circ)]\n= \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos 80^\\circ + \\cos(-60^\\circ)]\n= \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos 80^\\circ + \\cos 60^\\circ]\n\\]\n\nSince $\\cos 60^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2}$, we have:\n\\[\n\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 70^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos 80^\\circ + \\frac{1}{2}]\n= \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 80^\\circ + \\frac{1}{4}\n\\]\n\nStep 3: Multiply this result by $\\cos 50^\\circ$:\n\\[\n(\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 70^\\circ) \\cos 50^\\circ = \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 80^\\circ + \\frac{1}{4} \\right) \\cos 50^\\circ\n\\]\n\nUsing the distributive property:\n\\[\n= \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 80^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ + \\frac{1}{4} \\cos 50^\\circ\n\\]\n\nStep 4: Use the product-to-sum identity again on $\\cos 80^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ$:\n\\[\n\\cos 80^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos(80^\\circ + 50^\\circ) + \\cos(80^\\circ - 50^\\circ)]\n= \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos 130^\\circ + \\cos 30^\\circ]\n= \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos 130^\\circ + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}]\n\\]\n\nSince $\\cos 130^\\circ = -\\cos 50^\\circ$, we get:\n\\[\n\\cos 80^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} [-\\cos 50^\\circ + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}]\n= -\\frac{1}{2} \\cos 50^\\circ + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\n\\]\n\nStep 5: Substitute back into our expression:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2} \\left( -\\frac{1}{2} \\cos 50^\\circ + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\right) + \\frac{1}{4} \\cos 50^\\circ\n= -\\frac{1}{4} \\cos 50^\\circ + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} + \\frac{1}{4} \\cos 50^\\circ\n= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8}\n\\]\n\nStep 6: Multiply by $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\frac{3}{16}\n\\]\n\nThus, the simplified expression is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{3}{16}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{3}{16}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To simplify the expression $\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 30^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ \\cos 70^\\circ$, we can use trigonometric identities and properties.\n\nStep 1: Use the known value of $\\cos 30^\\circ$.\n\\[\n\\cos 30^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\nThus, the expression becomes:\n\\[\n\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ \\cos 70^\\circ \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nStep 2: Use the product-to-sum identities to simplify $\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ \\cos 70^\\circ$.\n\nWe can use the identity:\n\\[\n\\cos A \\cos B = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos(A+B) + \\cos(A-B)]\n\\]\n\nFirst, let's simplify the product $\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 70^\\circ$:\n\\[\n\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 70^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos(10^\\circ + 70^\\circ) + \\cos(10^\\circ - 70^\\circ)]\n= \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos 80^\\circ + \\cos(-60^\\circ)]\n= \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos 80^\\circ + \\cos 60^\\circ]\n\\]\n\nSince $\\cos 60^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2}$, we have:\n\\[\n\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 70^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos 80^\\circ + \\frac{1}{2}]\n= \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 80^\\circ + \\frac{1}{4}\n\\]\n\nStep 3: Multiply this result by $\\cos 50^\\circ$:\n\\[\n(\\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 70^\\circ) \\cos 50^\\circ = \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 80^\\circ + \\frac{1}{4} \\right) \\cos 50^\\circ\n\\]\n\nUsing the distributive property:\n\\[\n= \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 80^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ + \\frac{1}{4} \\cos 50^\\circ\n\\]\n\nStep 4: Use the product-to-sum identity again on $\\cos 80^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ$:\n\\[\n\\cos 80^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos(80^\\circ + 50^\\circ) + \\cos(80^\\circ - 50^\\circ)]\n= \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos 130^\\circ + \\cos 30^\\circ]\n= \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos 130^\\circ + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}]\n\\]\n\nSince $\\cos 130^\\circ = -\\cos 50^\\circ$, we get:\n\\[\n\\cos 80^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} [-\\cos 50^\\circ + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}]\n= -\\frac{1}{2} \\cos 50^\\circ + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\n\\]\n\nStep 5: Substitute back into our expression:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2} \\left( -\\frac{1}{2} \\cos 50^\\circ + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\right) + \\frac{1}{4} \\cos 50^\\circ\n= -\\frac{1}{4} \\cos 50^\\circ + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} + \\frac{1}{4} \\cos 50^\\circ\n= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8}\n\\]\n\nStep 6: Multiply by $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\frac{3}{16}\n\\]\n\nThus, the simplified expression is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{3}{16}}\n\\]","id":"precalculus:405"}
